ng a Falling Body Model With Linear Drag"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee9ad499-5aa4-5a3a-acbc-e49ac21406a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{kg/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391534} {:answer/id #uuid "b2955172-b81c-5011-9701-e9bf938ea274" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{s/m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391535} {:answer/id #uuid "decbd6c4-0fbe-5f80-9104-406758eb230d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391536} {:answer/id #uuid "1f5581a2-3f9c-5148-8b70-4faaa09845fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391537} {:answer/id #uuid "9195c8be-ff5f-5d44-bb08-28247be9de64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee9ad499-5aa4-5a3a-acbc-e49ac21406a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{kg/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391534} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4490809-5163-5471-a1d4-a55afc28a312" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391533} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50b58fcb-84d7-579f-880d-eb2423553ca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391540} {:answer/id #uuid "1b5d9fd5-a8df-5113-baf9-e036a0442b43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391541} {:answer/id #uuid "654583d2-f325-5d3b-9bf1-95a849ca6cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{4}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391542} {:answer/id #uuid "a003cd6c-ace7-5cea-850c-762788d62687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{5}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a003cd6c-ace7-5cea-850c-762788d62687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{5}v=g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391543} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cec6877-9346-5efe-a85d-d63bfc7cf07e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391539} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14031906-5df2-543f-a91e-f54dba551c97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391545} {:answer/id #uuid "69cee0c3-8008-5a0d-a9f2-37f86a67608c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391546} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc61919-e975-55cf-994c-bc8361ad199d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391547} {:answer/id #uuid "e24e6796-8ece-597a-9157-25358a0219a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391548} {:answer/id #uuid "0184e718-3d0a-5f0f-b11d-20a62daa4641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0184e718-3d0a-5f0f-b11d-20a62daa4641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391549} :answer-field/id #uuid "1502aa04-c760-583b-813d-02d5faca3f23" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "64f5cd17-fe8c-5f61-947e-78b369ea5b30" :question/math-academy-id "q-335179" :question/problem "A research container of mass $20\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the container's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the descent began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the container is proportional to its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k>0$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the container’s velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration is $8.2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nFind the constant of proportionality $k,$ and the differential equation that best models the velocity. Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the container are weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n$\\,$ The constant of proportionality is $k=$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ The differential equation that best models the velocity is\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, there are two forces acting on the body:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the body, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =20g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the velocity of the body (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-kv,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Note that, since the drag, in $N=\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ is the product of the constant $k$ and velocity, in $\\text{m/s},$ the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{N}}{\\text{m/s}}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2}}{\\text{m/s}}=\\text{kg/s}.\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the body is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=20g-kv.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the body, we have\n\n$$\n20g-kv=20\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}.\n$$\n\nWriting in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}+kv & =20g \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{k}{20}v & =g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we're given that, at a particular moment, the body's velocity is $8\\,\\text{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration is $8.2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\nSo, substituting $v=8,g=9.8,$ and $\\frac{dv}{dt}=8.2$ into our model, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8.2+\\frac{k}{20}\\cdot 8 & =9.8 \\\\ \\frac{2k}{5} & =1.6 \\\\ k & =4\\,\\text{kg/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation that models the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}+\\frac{1}{5}v=g.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d92507-9294-550b-bb70-20e6e46f340d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radially Projected Model for Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ef49603-a807-5f45-982c-373a15835b86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{r}-(1.6\\times {10}^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391551} {:answer/id #uuid "041f118d-162c-5545-bb0c-a3cba2bcf150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{1.15\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(1.6\\times {10}^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391552} {:answer/id #uuid "71fc6b2a-4a64-5b8d-aecb-dab45d68c6cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(1.6\\times {10}^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391553} {:answer/id #uuid "24e292a0-859a-569e-b479-5d6a968cab39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{1.15\\times {10}^{18}}{r}-(9.2\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391554} {:answer/id #uuid "639bf900-44e3-5ebc-86b0-d807c159046c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{{r}^{2}}+(9.2\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71fc6b2a-4a64-5b8d-aecb-dab45d68c6cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(1.6\\times {10}^{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391553} :answer-field/id #uuid "b31a8672-9812-5f3b-958f-3de2d774aebe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "575cae6c-4604-5b3a-b514-5f09e281fdca" :question/math-academy-id "q-335240" :question/problem "Consider a spherical planet with radius $(2.5\\times {10}^{8})\\,\\text{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g=18.4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Which equation best models the velocity $v$ of an object projected radially outward from the planet's surface with an initial velocity of $(9.6\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{m/s},$ where $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object. Assume the only force acting on the object is the force due to gravity." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\n{F}_{\\text{in}}=G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, and $M$ is the planet's mass.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-{F}_{\\text{in}}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance, \n\n$$\na=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\n with constant of proportionality $k=GM>0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R,$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n-g=-\\frac{k}{{R}^{2}}\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,k=g{R}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\frac{dv}{dr}=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int (-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}})\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{r})+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet ${v}_{0}$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R)={v}_{0},$ we can solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\n{v}^{2}=\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values\n$R=(2.5\\times {10}^{8}),$\n$g=18.4,$ and\n${v}_{0}=(9.6\\times {10}^{4}),$\nwe conclude that the equation for the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 18.4\\cdot (2.5\\times {10}^{8})^{2}}{r}+(9.6\\times {10}^{4})^{2}-2\\cdot 18.4\\cdot (2.5\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{230\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(92.16\\times {10}^{8})-(92\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+((92.16-92)\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(0.16\\times {10}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2.3\\times {10}^{18}}{r}+(1.6\\times {10}^{7}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb88ef39-e68a-5400-8035-7c504e8a0052" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Falling Body Model With Quadratic Drag"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53bf1247-4bdb-5860-b42d-8656e5488ccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\text{kg/m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391558} {:answer/id #uuid "978f19e1-cbad-5cfe-a4cf-327b1a0d2b9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{kg/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391559} {:answer/id #uuid "dd53c99c-6672-58d0-a8d7-6b6a85519ebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391560} {:answer/id #uuid "0860b0e5-98dc-56a5-8ca2-64bbdfb63f65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391561} {:answer/id #uuid "8f34c60b-cdcc-5649-992a-bd69cc725d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{kg/m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f34c60b-cdcc-5649-992a-bd69cc725d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{kg/m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391562} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d05016f-c6e8-5e68-9ad1-dbee81ade6b7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391557} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fe4445f-db0f-50e5-ba03-6f1709cf9c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g-\\frac{1}{5}{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391564} {:answer/id #uuid "306cbee3-c94b-585f-94d9-a472be237c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g-\\frac{1}{10}{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391565} {:answer/id #uuid "93d19470-28e7-5b64-8ead-2b5ec5682b30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g-\\frac{1}{20}{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391566} {:answer/id #uuid "faf5631b-4c0c-5495-b711-f25a88a01f5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g-\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fe4445f-db0f-50e5-ba03-6f1709cf9c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g-\\frac{1}{5}{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391564} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ae5433b-8bd2-50d7-acda-33a7b8dcf837" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391563} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c96f0a3c-b9e0-58ff-a881-757b94a3f933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391569} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc697ab-b569-5755-8c51-6ffef423e6d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391570} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7bbe00-0586-5ded-9b11-92eb11e8cff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391571} {:answer/id #uuid "ed06a57c-8178-5e79-b84a-aa2614071bee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391572} {:answer/id #uuid "0905afcd-4e83-523c-8528-005a197029b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cc697ab-b569-5755-8c51-6ffef423e6d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391570} :answer-field/id #uuid "36c05b26-01eb-544c-8831-399aed0d6baa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "53da92ca-2c6d-5b4b-91fd-641e504d29e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-335390" :question/problem "A container of mass $10\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the body's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the fall began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the container is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k>0$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the container's velocity is $5\\,\\text{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration is $4.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nFind the constant of proportionality $k,$ and the differential equation that best models the velocity.\nAssume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the container are weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n$\\,$ The constant of proportionality is $k=$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ The differential equation that best models the velocity is $\\frac{dv}{dt}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, two forces are acting on the container:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the container, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =10g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the square of the velocity of the container (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Note that, since drag, in $\\text{N}=\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ is the product of the constant $k$ and squared velocity, in $(\\text{m/s})^{2}={\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{N}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2}}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2}}=\\text{kg/m}.\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the container is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=10g-k{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the container, we get\n\n$$\n10g-k{v}^{2}=10\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{k}{10}{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we're given that, at a particular moment, the container's velocity is $5\\,\\text{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration is $4.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ So, substituting $v=5,g=9.8,$ and $\\frac{dv}{dt}=4.8$ into our model, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4.8 & =9.8-\\frac{k}{10}\\cdot {5}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}k & =5 \\\\ k & =2\\,\\text{kg/m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the differential equation that models the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{1}{5}{v}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bbeb4f6-23b2-586c-8cb9-9742cce5a9a9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bd0b65e-3a0f-5994-9689-9aef5b5c9454" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{-x}+C{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391576} {:answer/id #uuid "3fae5784-6514-5371-820c-1909539d4c72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{e}^{2x}+C{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391577} {:answer/id #uuid "58c34fda-7cad-508d-a7f2-d8bf06c08b7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{e}^{-2x}+C{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391578} {:answer/id #uuid "7448e8d2-a22f-59f6-bc6b-c65f6e7cf2de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{2x}+C{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7448e8d2-a22f-59f6-bc6b-c65f6e7cf2de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{2x}+C{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391579} :answer-field/id #uuid "d308949f-707a-5a2c-802c-a0a17c33433a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391575} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4e6cf3c-fccb-5029-b604-d1593b0daddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391581} {:answer/id #uuid "1e61f74a-48e8-542c-a33f-c9fe7a376f8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391582} {:answer/id #uuid "74653977-5fc1-57a5-a3e9-24eb70eb33c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391583} {:answer/id #uuid "fb69fbee-2b0a-5d6f-b11d-267cf756dd9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74653977-5fc1-57a5-a3e9-24eb70eb33c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391583} :answer-field/id #uuid "2021aa17-0151-5e2d-987a-a1856edc5e53" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6530e5db-b521-5f5c-99f7-59872d852e59" :question/math-academy-id "q-335402" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=6{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is already in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=-3$ and $Q(x)=6{e}^{2x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{-3x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y) & ={e}^{-3x}\\cdot 6{e}^{2x} \\\\ {e}^{-3x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{e}^{-3x}\\cdot y & =6{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-3x}) & =6{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-3x})\\text{d}x & =\\int 6{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{-3x} & =-6{e}^{-x}+C \\\\ y & =-6{e}^{2x}+C{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e58bb6-6d90-5171-bba2-6ffa5a0c91d9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem Using a Given Matrix Exponential: Shifted Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3c3294a-f087-5672-a53e-117506420382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391587} {:answer/id #uuid "601ea9e6-24b0-535e-bb17-49ca178db97e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391588} {:answer/id #uuid "59b758f5-6067-571d-908b-9e50262c9da7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391589} {:answer/id #uuid "66384a82-a5a2-5020-8b9b-8db002bae474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391590} {:answer/id #uuid "70ed0219-7267-5410-9f26-31afb335cb12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66384a82-a5a2-5020-8b9b-8db002bae474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391590} :answer-field/id #uuid "afd92e9b-c3b2-5f27-ae61-1167e9f32949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ab60b60-d1a6-5ef2-aaf8-6579001f22b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335448" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(1) \\\\ y(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1)) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e58bb6-6d90-5171-bba2-6ffa5a0c91d9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem Using a Given Matrix Exponential: Shifted Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "942a629e-94b8-5c24-bbcc-97e7d789cb40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t+1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t+1))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391594} {:answer/id #uuid "9b04c091-3bbb-5dae-9ed9-1a467ab29d95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t+1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t+1))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391595} {:answer/id #uuid "07f2c784-653a-5833-9229-3fb331cf19a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t+1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t+1))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391596} {:answer/id #uuid "4ea30351-9279-59bc-8f85-f17f0652612d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t+1)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t+1))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391597} {:answer/id #uuid "150d70c1-d912-530b-b05b-4c87bea0ff00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t+1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t+1))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "150d70c1-d912-530b-b05b-4c87bea0ff00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t+1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t+1))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391598} :answer-field/id #uuid "36126579-e63c-590b-a6f7-f58bde2b64a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6580aa1b-3c6f-5533-9e32-e7afb350be95" :question/math-academy-id "q-335450" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(-1) \\\\ y(-1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-(-1))}\\,x(-1) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t+1)) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t+1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t+1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t+1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t+1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t+1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01c5809f-cb10-5192-b646-13036813137d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bb9195b-384d-5b2f-bad0-6dd43e0f4b44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391601} {:answer/id #uuid "f34ac63a-04a2-52a7-a90f-f8f4d8d09299" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}-2){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391602} {:answer/id #uuid "2d29d24c-fd03-58f5-91b9-3629bcaeee91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391603} {:answer/id #uuid "f585add5-def6-5238-932b-3a30184083f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2){e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391604} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f4e1e7-75e7-58c7-87b8-4747684ab781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}-2){e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3f4e1e7-75e7-58c7-87b8-4747684ab781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}-2){e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391605} :answer-field/id #uuid "7806b0bb-9404-54a9-9afe-9fe5aae500c4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391600} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d49da7f-4559-53d4-88e7-a4a538e83bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391607} {:answer/id #uuid "185130ca-9e63-5f95-8858-fbe1360a02af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391608} {:answer/id #uuid "24752170-0362-5a79-8550-d496d2deb223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391609} {:answer/id #uuid "7644849e-bdfd-532f-b35d-2dd327bd7b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d49da7f-4559-53d4-88e7-a4a538e83bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391607} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a5f76ae-1afb-592e-a102-26dd10e20b42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c6ef87c-1a0b-5973-8a1d-74f719874948" :question/math-academy-id "q-335806" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=2x{e}^{-x},\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=1$ and $Q(x)=2x{e}^{-x}.$ \n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={e}^{x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot 2x{e}^{-x} \\\\ {e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{x}y & =2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x}) & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x})\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{x} & ={x}^{2}+C \\\\ y & =({x}^{2}+C){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=-2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =({0}^{2}+C){e}^{-0} \\\\ -2 & =(0+C)\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=({x}^{2}-2){e}^{-x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01c5809f-cb10-5192-b646-13036813137d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13c71556-c8bb-509e-b7b6-813b830221cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391613} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc0fd51-b3d5-5b60-822b-55ce1a39a048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391614} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5f9c6d-a3bd-55c8-a595-021440af2d20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-5{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391615} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff86545-6fe9-59ce-a43b-d596ae985d85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391616} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e86c18-962f-5498-8cd8-fd15eaf09b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13c71556-c8bb-509e-b7b6-813b830221cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391613} :answer-field/id #uuid "75c53fba-addc-5347-89bc-46926b4cec8a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391612} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68eff5b7-73e9-589d-b262-8718bbf64cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391619} {:answer/id #uuid "708406f2-8554-5163-8a8c-780da5f0a24e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391620} {:answer/id #uuid "38c2efa8-509c-59df-90e0-5ff1a247796d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391621} {:answer/id #uuid "878ffcb1-ce95-5b25-bcf5-982716631d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38c2efa8-509c-59df-90e0-5ff1a247796d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391621} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0dfecd2-1e22-5e5a-aff3-e35469e93c9a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cba95378-fbb4-59fe-bb7c-3f501d3e7ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335829" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=8,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=4$ and $Q(x)=8.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={e}^{4x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y) & ={e}^{4x}\\cdot 8 \\\\ {e}^{4x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4{e}^{4x}\\cdot y & =8{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{4x}) & =8{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{4x})\\text{d}x & =\\int 8{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{4x} & =8(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4x})+C \\\\ y{e}^{4x} & =2{e}^{4x}+C \\\\ y & =2+C{e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =2+C{e}^{-4(0)} \\\\ -1 & =2+C(1) \\\\ C & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2-3{e}^{-4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ff1822c-afad-54f8-aa36-7e0de79d9d04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f60c71f-f1a0-5dc9-bc85-bc53e3b5d9e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,j+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391625} {:answer/id #uuid "2f82adaf-7d79-546e-b51a-3d2064321d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j+\\frac{1}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391626} {:answer/id #uuid "10b01e1d-27d8-5784-b829-e7428d99b479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391627} {:answer/id #uuid "8b39b63d-e746-538f-8858-f3d5381d09d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,j+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391628} {:answer/id #uuid "6560a5a6-5578-53c6-881e-58e2deb05371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f82adaf-7d79-546e-b51a-3d2064321d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j+\\frac{1}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391626} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d43ad92-b445-5432-aa1f-111fa6dcfbd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93a1c03f-77b3-5fa3-b1a1-22418386cc31" :question/math-academy-id "q-33584" :question/problem "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=2t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+t\\,k$ at the point $P(2,1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,j+y(t)\\,j+z(t)\\,k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by \n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,1,1)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i+\\,j+\\,k \\\\ 2t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+t\\,k & =2\\,i+\\,j+\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2t=2 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ t=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(2,1,1)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2t\\,j+\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =2\\,i+2(1)\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(2,1,1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3}\\,j+\\frac{1}{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391630 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d92507-9294-550b-bb70-20e6e46f340d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Radially Projected Model for Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9460548-1670-5748-96e7-bc24a48140dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{1.92\\times {10}^{16}}{r}-(6.4\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391632} {:answer/id #uuid "7567d868-8018-5a56-ba2a-c4d82ce4076c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{3.84\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(5.44\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391633} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7dc8e9-09fb-5583-bb8b-d85f5b039614" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{3.84\\times {10}^{16}}{{r}^{2}}+(6.4\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391634} {:answer/id #uuid "65a06374-c496-5f30-a16a-4775df0ecb09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{3.84\\times {10}^{16}}{r}-(5.44\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391635} {:answer/id #uuid "e307943a-6dce-567a-ba81-fc41c27b1ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{1.92\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(5.44\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7567d868-8018-5a56-ba2a-c4d82ce4076c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}=\\frac{3.84\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(5.44\\times {10}^{9})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391633} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef392484-5295-5810-8c60-385d592168de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3934db8-2760-5a29-9e9a-8bb1a5f5607d" :question/math-academy-id "q-336341" :question/problem "Consider a spherical planet with radius $(4\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{m}$ and surface gravitational acceleration $g=12\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Which equation best models the velocity $v$ of an object projected radially outward from the planet's surface with an initial velocity of $(8\\times {10}^{4})\\,\\text{m/s},$ where $r$ is the distance from the planet's center to the object. Assume the only force acting on the object is the force due to gravity." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the planet's radius and $g$ be the planet's surface gravitational acceleration.\n\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\n{F}_{\\text{in}}=G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, and $M$ is the planet's mass.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-{F}_{\\text{in}}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance,\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwith constant of proportionality $k=GM>0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=R,$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-g.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n-g=-\\frac{k}{{R}^{2}}\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,k=g{R}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\frac{dv}{dr}=-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSolving using separation of variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int (-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}})\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{r})+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C=2{C}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nLet ${v}_{0}$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R)={v}_{0},$ we can solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity of the object is\n\n$$\n{v}^{2}=\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+{v}_{0}^{2}-2gR.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting in the values\n$R=(4\\times {10}^{7}),$\n$g=12,$ and\n${v}_{0}=(8\\times {10}^{4}),$\nwe conclude that the equation for the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 12\\cdot (4\\times {10}^{7})^{2}}{r}+(8\\times {10}^{4})^{2}-2\\cdot 12\\cdot (4\\times {10}^{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{384\\times {10}^{14}}{r}+(64\\times {10}^{8})-(96\\times {10}^{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3.84\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(6.4\\times {10}^{9})-(0.96\\times {10}^{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3.84\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+((6.4-0.96)\\times {10}^{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3.84\\times {10}^{16}}{r}+(5.44\\times {10}^{9}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c20cd9a-5734-5129-93db-55f6dc985b3a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Formulas for Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a40fb41-904d-581f-9085-4a80f508ca4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by $Φ^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164748} {:answer/id #uuid "54cc4d7e-999e-535e-8b7f-829d5f8a361b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164749} {:answer/id #uuid "620f0ef2-de67-5ea0-be3f-b1a901e676fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "adding $Φ$ to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164750} {:answer/id #uuid "752ec359-a9d2-569a-b4ba-305559870367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164751}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0e207189-d64b-57c9-893d-c96318522060" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164747} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98517f60-dd1c-5e01-ac7f-a08c5c8558c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is an invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164753} {:answer/id #uuid "f5307e2a-39c8-5b17-b307-b9e8d5590c6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a fundamental" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164754} {:answer/id #uuid "0a8ddf49-18f6-5121-bbd9-fa8e15f3c65d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164755} {:answer/id #uuid "69f0b669-e69d-5b75-9e55-a76a16dd41a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is the identity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164756}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b5c81b57-321a-5219-99a2-c7fccb4eb606" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164752} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e418c22f-ba5a-5401-b5b9-e001ed1eacf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "initial system of ODEs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164758} {:answer/id #uuid "f40d19bd-5ae5-5255-9b77-cb620368916d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "first shifting theorem" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164759} {:answer/id #uuid "105bcac5-8b8e-5e9a-9580-6251dbee6962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "line $L4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164760} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0bb450-af31-5c71-bdf7-8f54ec8705cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "solution of the system of ODEs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164761}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bc8d1671-5b28-55b4-94cf-4636d83491ce" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164757} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "190f80f0-e123-5399-934c-1177d892e721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "adding $Φ$ to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164763} {:answer/id #uuid "1a4c5e67-5d3f-5c7c-945b-73bfd5ada9af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164764} {:answer/id #uuid "61c40f64-a096-5b92-b47d-bd4620a11ecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164765} {:answer/id #uuid "7debf8c9-b398-50ee-aa43-2e1201ef8982" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by $Φ^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164766}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1ae1cb92-929c-5a34-a92c-301d276151cb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164762}] :question/id #uuid "f3e98464-6e85-513e-a930-d0947c1c72b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-336711" :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n$x^{′}(t) = Ax(t) + f(t)$\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form $x_{p}(t) = Φu(t)$, where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for $x_{p}(t)$ is given below.\n\n> $L1$: $x_{p}(t) = Φu(t)$\n> $L2$: $x_{p}^{′}(t) = Φu^{′}(t) + Φ^{′}u(t)$\n> $L3$: $Φu^{′}(t) + Φ^{′}u(t) = AΦu(t) + f(t)$\n> $L4$: $Φ^{′} = AΦ$\n> $L5$: $Φu^{′}(t) = f(t)$\n> $L6$: $u^{′}(t) = Φ^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n> $L7$: $u(t) = \\displaystyle \\intΦ^{-1}f(t)dt$\n> $L8$: $x_{p}(t) = Φ\\displaystyle \\intΦ^{-1}f(t)dt$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from line $L1$ by {{field-1}} both sides of the equation.\n\nLine $L3$ is obtained by substituting $L1$ and $L2$ into the {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L6$ follows from line $L5$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from the fact that {{field-4}} matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc0992a-423e-5023-98d5-9d53c3b77788" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e513d59-ec4b-56e0-a149-748a6164fa0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391639} {:answer/id #uuid "5b08fcdf-1c61-56cb-b330-8a9759c8a89e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391640} {:answer/id #uuid "8210e913-8fc4-50f7-87cd-8fd56787dfec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391641} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b489bc-4e90-59af-8076-7c8a1e2ef20d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391642} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c3feb9-80f0-5263-bb9b-e4994b84169c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e513d59-ec4b-56e0-a149-748a6164fa0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391639} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ffd0d69-a63d-58b1-a22c-7c7b302f6577" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "214d5c25-f05c-5b96-87cf-f66e123da8aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-33706" :question/problem "![](images/10/10ee30ddf508bf985e1c3a41491818723b57301412a5514e4a39c3bc13518acd.png)\n\nWhich integral  expression gives the arc length of the curve $g(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,2-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=\\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${g}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{g}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a016197-61af-57f3-9c3a-05410db1374d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inexact Differential Equations Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5aa962c-7d0e-52df-8d52-ef49cea07bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{y}^{4}+3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391645} {:answer/id #uuid "11e3d474-20b2-5032-9666-95b4f01e1179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391646} {:answer/id #uuid "f75b222d-003d-5187-8076-ece9e06f1717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x{y}^{2}+xy=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391647} {:answer/id #uuid "73f61c2c-6548-57e3-8b11-917ede06e5e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}+y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11e3d474-20b2-5032-9666-95b4f01e1179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391646} :answer-field/id #uuid "95895bc2-016a-540f-933b-27ed94e47a3c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391644} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2e30145-5610-556c-b3c9-6712ae5787c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391650} {:answer/id #uuid "b181fa3b-5355-508d-8687-40cf1538a9e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391651} {:answer/id #uuid "04f7ffac-fd58-5761-bb3a-04a54c62e4bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391652} {:answer/id #uuid "1ecf71d4-8f46-5451-a460-3ca8dfbc5fc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b181fa3b-5355-508d-8687-40cf1538a9e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391651} :answer-field/id #uuid "85d87871-391a-5f75-8927-010186af7a6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1effee9c-dd60-525b-b725-817b354982d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-337096" :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n(\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int 3{x}^{-1}\\text{ }\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}[(\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{(6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{(4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y=c.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fc0992a-423e-5023-98d5-9d53c3b77788" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc3dbeb4-85f0-5977-97bc-032932c84bc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡2t+9{sin}^{2}⁡3t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391655} {:answer/id #uuid "769deadf-6f4e-5979-ab29-41e31ac33156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡2t+9{cos}^{2}⁡3t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391656} {:answer/id #uuid "8acb4dd0-ab7b-50a4-bed8-752a2b64b388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡2t+9{sin}^{2}⁡3t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391657} {:answer/id #uuid "4b968ecc-eb2e-5899-bdd8-779a4c1f182d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡2t+3{sin}^{2}⁡3t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391658} {:answer/id #uuid "0ec6159d-f7de-5b2f-b124-7ba4b0ab6ebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡2t+9{cos}^{2}⁡3t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8acb4dd0-ab7b-50a4-bed8-752a2b64b388" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡2t+9{sin}^{2}⁡3t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391657} :answer-field/id #uuid "438187f3-2ea9-5adc-8e76-df9ae09801fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "994d254c-8dfb-50db-8abf-3c167a677836" :question/math-academy-id "q-33728" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d162245ac19c3ef9ae63d602fb4e188ddfd241a1a604304f6c272c3902ce1e93.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the arc length of the curve $g(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩$ from $t=0$ to $t=\\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${g}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{g}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡2t+9{sin}^{2}⁡3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡2t+9{sin}^{2}⁡3t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391660 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a016197-61af-57f3-9c3a-05410db1374d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Inexact Differential Equations Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "405a5785-5b34-57dd-8ee6-7cf3c14c3b4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391662} {:answer/id #uuid "02c9e893-9d91-5235-b549-382b71a5543f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391663} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc97e04-8bc1-5d35-bd81-38e3fca740d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391664} {:answer/id #uuid "1e9976bc-fa6f-5d35-a147-d160f45c0036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcc97e04-8bc1-5d35-bd81-38e3fca740d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391664} :answer-field/id #uuid "20192922-8171-54fa-90a2-ddc855fb0da5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391661} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a7efa86-b472-5ed5-b224-1426be141770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+3y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391667} {:answer/id #uuid "28cb4cfd-dc4e-58e9-9682-e66719264ada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391668} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2aa6bc-5f1f-5b98-a317-03dc20826446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+3y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391669} {:answer/id #uuid "bdcc8170-82d1-59e4-8e50-50028abda132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+3y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdcc8170-82d1-59e4-8e50-50028abda132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+3y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391670} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb50e09f-3243-5b20-805e-de37af2e1718" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5b12f52-bca9-597a-88b5-c5036d3e9da3" :question/math-academy-id "q-338027" :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n(3x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+\\frac{3}{x})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=3x+{y}^{2},\\,Q(x,y)=xy+\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int {x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x[(3x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+\\frac{3}{x})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ {{(3{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{({x}^{2}y+3)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)={x}^{2}y+3.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (3{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}y+3)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+3y+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=3y,\\,G(x)={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+3y=c.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e12488e1-7367-534c-b671-45ac946491b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-x{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391673} {:answer/id #uuid "1dfc005d-b8c5-5d1c-acfb-3fd452089861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391674} {:answer/id #uuid "a51ecfa7-fa52-5029-911c-dc5479ded457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391675} {:answer/id #uuid "3c38a64e-abff-5a2a-ba7b-a1d3529f9fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a51ecfa7-fa52-5029-911c-dc5479ded457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391675} :answer-field/id #uuid "9de66f5a-151d-5fb8-b56f-c3a5d5e2c5f4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391672} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1633c85-3e38-5846-8e2c-979722f33f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}-x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391678} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c178d7-d5d2-55f6-b571-6cba27959a4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x{e}^{3x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391679} {:answer/id #uuid "8364c88e-1c7b-5b13-8b70-fc1e2ce0e96d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}-\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391680} {:answer/id #uuid "fc30ff3f-a67f-5372-af7d-0b91fc8ac3c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8c178d7-d5d2-55f6-b571-6cba27959a4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x{e}^{3x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391679} :answer-field/id #uuid "55743caa-314e-5ff9-bd5b-d898cf65473b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391677} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbc5bac8-5b7a-5c19-93f1-bae2fe479b85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391683} {:answer/id #uuid "1e5f487f-97de-525a-8ef2-78de862ebab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391684} {:answer/id #uuid "66e1eac2-e838-5246-8845-592955592eaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391685} {:answer/id #uuid "31e15c80-d4f7-5e0b-bf4b-be5d0a06121f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391686} {:answer/id #uuid "1eaab4ea-e2c4-5234-8f9c-b2aa93dfe18c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e5f487f-97de-525a-8ef2-78de862ebab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391684} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8a624c7-1e30-5578-a91d-1a6b7e1db452" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30381914-6c05-5474-8e6f-cee39aeb98f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-338129" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x=\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x \\\\  & =6{e}^{-3x}(\\frac{{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}}{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3x}({e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =3+3{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =3+3{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3+3{e}^{-6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =3x-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =3x{e}^{3x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62196ae9-df2e-5a3a-bc0a-1c0f4c40af3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector To a Plane Given by Parametric Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23e02ac0-3e89-5c73-aee7-9f2de3148d7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391690} {:answer/id #uuid "8ffc566d-8d4c-53c3-af4b-b2d7a4d35946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391691} {:answer/id #uuid "efc39529-4f0f-5e00-b796-0c632007c03e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391692} {:answer/id #uuid "f83846d5-42d7-540e-ad5c-05350b13e9fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-2,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391693} {:answer/id #uuid "0693f405-c3d1-5022-a785-4b3d44119374" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23e02ac0-3e89-5c73-aee7-9f2de3148d7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,2,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391690} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aebbae6-4b97-5e3f-9a17-15682100023d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b07ac22-3309-5bc0-ac4b-6e1be0e5a45e" :question/math-academy-id "q-33823" :question/problem "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\nr=⟨1,1,0⟩+t⟨-1,1,1⟩+s⟨0,2,2⟩,\\,t,\\,s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨-1,1,1⟩$ and $w=⟨0,2,2⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,2,-2⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af8cf798-4327-5f5b-82ab-f622f20ccc38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "944bf8ed-46b7-55b7-9ae7-f3686f03d94d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149920} {:answer/id #uuid "3b51d554-2a7e-5113-a1a9-745a3657fa28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149921}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2f42c2ad-4295-5c76-9034-69c1ed9b17f2" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149919} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b4d8744-68d8-54ae-a159-8c21bede4028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149923} {:answer/id #uuid "494865e3-dfc1-541f-9ff5-5d9f025d0fac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149924}] :answer-field/id #uuid "96f4839c-09e1-50b0-aed6-3bdd704ddc14" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149922} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da9aa752-15cc-53a4-9162-21052fdd6da0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149926} {:answer/id #uuid "414fd097-359f-555a-a1cb-cbb37664e91f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149927}] :answer-field/id #uuid "73fc3e34-e0bc-5850-b9c8-10f5bb3ae547" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149925} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2443c50-f862-5df4-9df6-1d1704f55fdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Robin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149929} {:answer/id #uuid "3b7bf673-832e-554f-abad-c9c2bf816133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Neumann" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149930} {:answer/id #uuid "57499eb1-9f19-50a3-b6ff-7b2d72e110f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dirichlet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149931}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e0cd74a7-381b-5ae9-b084-02522f2dcd22" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149928}] :question/id #uuid "b711ebb4-4d84-521d-8e27-4cb308558d9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-338516" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y^{″}(x) + x^{2}y^{′}(x) + y(x) = 2x, 0 \\le x \\le π$\n\n$2y(0) + y^{′}(0) = 0, y(π) + 2y^{′}(π) = 0$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\nThe governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\nFor this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\nThe boundary value problem is {{field-4}}."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7343e3-fa7d-58c8-b4c7-49ef5b85110b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Tangent Given the Parametrization of a Curve by Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "709b5f28-0e29-5ad2-bab0-b92ea1a1b0f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})i-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})j+\\frac{3}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391696} {:answer/id #uuid "b97152d5-41a8-5677-bbe8-d87732e9a490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})i+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})j+\\frac{3}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391697} {:answer/id #uuid "98f50433-2881-5fa5-ab16-e8f9256eb1f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})i-80\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})j+\\frac{3}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391698} {:answer/id #uuid "8e664a17-2d65-5d9b-86ab-7a84885a6057" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=-80\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})i+80\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})j+\\frac{3}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391699} {:answer/id #uuid "2598ae05-2897-5893-88b7-0076255b4c61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10s)\\,i+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10s)\\,j+\\frac{3}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b97152d5-41a8-5677-bbe8-d87732e9a490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})i+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})j+\\frac{3}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391697} :answer-field/id #uuid "048f13ed-3065-59f6-8bd1-afd74a547268" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7af7c919-7113-54d4-9f83-a2e6f1bdbb45" :question/math-academy-id "q-33861" :question/problem "Given the curve  $r(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+6t\\,k,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=10t,$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $s=10t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{10}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{10}) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})i+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})j+6(\\frac{s}{10})k \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})i+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})j+\\frac{3s}{5}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{s}{10})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{s}{10})j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3s}{5})k \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})i+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})j+\\frac{3}{5}\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})i+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{s}{10})j+\\frac{3}{5}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af8cf798-4327-5f5b-82ab-f622f20ccc38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "938283d2-beda-511f-aecc-58595635904b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149933} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9dabe7-7b7f-58e1-8fc8-b3ce980ec136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149934}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b4c80d90-6494-52f2-89ea-dc293115cce6" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149932} {:answer