1712} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c87594-7fd9-5262-b977-690b21e75561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391713} {:answer/id #uuid "56ed424b-8ca9-59d2-8528-1ebfe5b468f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{{\\lambda }_{n}x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6c87594-7fd9-5262-b977-690b21e75561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391713} :answer-field/id #uuid "d664b659-e57c-53ea-8b4c-aa060927c521" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b03c659-cc6c-5259-9cfb-c53b896cc809" :question/math-academy-id "q-338909" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter value corresponding to the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda x)+4B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+4B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda \\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n4\\lambda \\pi =n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{n}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22" :knowledge-point/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66f6bef1-778e-54ac-8bca-389bd469b61a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391717} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8245c8-0e8a-5ec6-812c-40fb520a0dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{400}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391718} {:answer/id #uuid "8091ad43-a6d6-542a-9238-3300819d2ba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391719} {:answer/id #uuid "dae1120f-db9d-59df-ac9c-c5067e0542fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b8245c8-0e8a-5ec6-812c-40fb520a0dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{400}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391718} :answer-field/id #uuid "77dfda2b-2332-5cb1-bf2d-c957e336718a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391716} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d90220b1-6fc2-5f68-a111-69d4ae63b772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391722} {:answer/id #uuid "0973d359-8928-55e0-aa49-7281d37dd42f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391723} {:answer/id #uuid "b7595082-877b-5b1f-81f8-341af224951c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391724} {:answer/id #uuid "137f89ad-4c21-5fc6-a296-ef6fbb06f150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7595082-877b-5b1f-81f8-341af224951c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391724} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8c60513-d952-5f41-b9e9-c5398ae1b7aa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72f2d583-8ffc-50a1-bdb5-e2d5a11cd167" :question/math-academy-id "q-338916" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16\\lambda y=0,\\,y(0)=y(5)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 5,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=y(5)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(5)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(20\\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n20\\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{400}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391726 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22" :knowledge-point/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d299aab6-7d45-5daf-aeed-dbcaf20eb01f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391728} {:answer/id #uuid "50cf2632-5224-53c3-9780-700b593c95e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391729} {:answer/id #uuid "dad65025-c4f6-50bb-bfc0-81fdab4d27f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391730} {:answer/id #uuid "78ab4f16-e632-5d7c-a0ed-fca61eb6d86b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}\\pi }{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dad65025-c4f6-50bb-bfc0-81fdab4d27f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391730} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3e4d0a8-336d-51eb-9840-10ac5a3bc5ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391727} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "950d017d-4409-5a7d-93e6-82c04aebf61f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391733} {:answer/id #uuid "a76caf84-a8f6-5ed7-b166-b53efe8b63f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391734} {:answer/id #uuid "7e019b9a-dbcd-5aaa-9d4a-e9839bb29f61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391735} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae13067-ee42-5ad2-b168-11769b5a9ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e019b9a-dbcd-5aaa-9d4a-e9839bb29f61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391735} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf084e4c-8bde-52e0-8415-918ddbdb9317" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55c03286-efa5-5f72-9411-1febab9b4b0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338920" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16\\lambda y=0,\\,y(0)=y(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=y(\\pi )=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n4\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{16}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bbeb4f6-23b2-586c-8cb9-9742cce5a9a9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d5e5efa-0ad9-5cb1-8007-def75d4c0f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391739} {:answer/id #uuid "181ce215-60ee-5a98-8d16-1f87a5c66bf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391740} {:answer/id #uuid "c201762a-3b3f-52e3-a4c2-6f449ab4bd0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391741} {:answer/id #uuid "45bdee38-54f6-5e18-9b5f-1f916081c4c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45bdee38-54f6-5e18-9b5f-1f916081c4c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391742} :answer-field/id #uuid "b52979ca-88b2-53be-b424-0a20f99bf0c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391738} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02bcb740-d3b0-5e03-ac52-deeb78de3ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{-3x}}{2}+C{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391744} {:answer/id #uuid "0f0496f9-803e-5f66-af97-d84ac1ef5ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}}{2}+C{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391745} {:answer/id #uuid "917a7312-27b6-560b-80be-1afb23076cab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{-3x}}{2}+C{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391746} {:answer/id #uuid "94fc55df-9a81-5694-9adb-ccac511991c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}}{2}+C{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94fc55df-9a81-5694-9adb-ccac511991c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}}{2}+C{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391747} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e39085e-c8a9-5cbc-b6d2-646a14b27243" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08d92346-a7e8-58c6-8d60-e4702d743dd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-339138" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=x{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=-3$ and $Q(x)=x{e}^{3x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{-3x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y) & ={e}^{-3x}\\cdot x{e}^{3x} \\\\ {e}^{-3x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{e}^{-3x}\\cdot y & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-3x}) & =x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-3x})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{-3x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}}{2}+C{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7132a908-b9cb-56e3-80b1-38fb5d14b3a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,1⟩)\\cdot ⟨-1,-1,16⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391749} {:answer/id #uuid "02494726-37b0-5e7b-ad3c-0180bececdfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-4⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,4⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391750} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ff3e24-bbb2-547d-9f0d-bbf347c313df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-1⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,0,4⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391751} {:answer/id #uuid "ef59ef21-d4e1-5b2a-82ab-a424aa93aa72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,4⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,4⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391752} {:answer/id #uuid "875a9589-3b53-5b62-afd7-0ad0dc57bbe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,1⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,4⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02494726-37b0-5e7b-ad3c-0180bececdfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-4⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,4⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391750} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f0ed6f5-6be8-59e5-9f66-3f6c05ef700b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d949e3c-e35b-5754-bfa5-04230aa782a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-33923" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $x-y+4z+16=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨1,-1,4⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y+4z+16 & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{1}{4}y-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-4.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-4)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,-4⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-4⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,4⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dfdbdba-247c-5ddd-bb6e-5d9637140264" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Linear System of Differential Equations in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0835bce8-cf59-5379-9f90-423f30051fa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391756} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3ac905-af6b-502c-8155-adc0cb0b600f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391757} {:answer/id #uuid "a58ec671-2cae-516a-aa76-7a23dc0a8a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -7 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391758} {:answer/id #uuid "98c7b93e-b65a-5f55-b235-d06a74563bf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391759} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6b6f59-1519-59c8-89a5-c6439694e171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98c7b93e-b65a-5f55-b235-d06a74563bf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391759} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ff9e82e-407b-5703-8567-eba193135c21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55e611bb-e159-5679-8a20-82ebbfb84458" :question/math-academy-id "q-339368" :question/problem "If the following system of differential equations is written in the form ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then what is the matrix $A?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)+7{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)+4{x}_{1}(t)-8{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nSuch a system can always be written in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a(t) & b(t) \\\\ c(t) & d(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ g(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have the following system.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)+7{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)+4{x}_{1}(t)-8{x}_{2}(t)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-7{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-4{x}_{1}(t)+8{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can rewrite our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Slope Fields Corresponding to Some Equilibrium Point Classifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "398b93a7-fe9b-53e5-a5b0-41feadd2a366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/92/9295a422a3f393012ebeb664e1b5867483b34e7f35260994364add19a1e99b48.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391763} {:answer/id #uuid "52e7d237-73ac-56b2-9a1a-0e1c5d5f0361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0b/0b8561b868005f359ec3a9d023ada261053fe04e8b3c970fc787a93faff91a5f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391764} {:answer/id #uuid "47ab71bf-4af1-58e9-b39a-4883b7f80dcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b0/b0435deaf5351bf3d0c5debe40ef705b8f8b01b6fd4133a2fbca4a12034d9b91.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391765} {:answer/id #uuid "c18a10ab-143f-533b-a132-1db3fe6d30a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/26/26f65c17a5b108cc0c739bbfa5d7971841bfe71dc8886fb801e2ca5b5d2a32ae.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391766} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9d9599-8516-5a4b-81f1-e87b85d3aac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ad/ad543fe5e3bda9c57f298c96547e722fea5bed48a4752cac1520b9814b11fa48.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c18a10ab-143f-533b-a132-1db3fe6d30a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/26/26f65c17a5b108cc0c739bbfa5d7971841bfe71dc8886fb801e2ca5b5d2a32ae.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391766} :answer-field/id #uuid "b113387d-78c1-5771-a337-db1596e8b663" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "293e0208-526f-5eaf-bcc8-3bedd1343359" :question/math-academy-id "q-340358" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-3$ is a node\n\n- $y=1$ is a source\n\n- $y=4$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-3,$ $y=1,$ and $y=4.$\n\n- We're given that $y=4$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=4$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=1$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=1$ (already drawn by the above property) and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-3$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=-3$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just above $y=-3$ points downward, the arrow just below it points downward too.\n\n![](images/e4/e428cf67a486e9eb17764727ab042bb44e07dfe4f13c125112fb56799d5aadea.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that corresponds with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/26/26f65c17a5b108cc0c739bbfa5d7971841bfe71dc8886fb801e2ca5b5d2a32ae.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-3,$ $y=1,$ and $y=4.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-3$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking below the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking above the line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=1$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=4$ appear to converge toward this line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "304c015d-cbb6-5989-a102-d6f0f5f38604" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Expressions For Fourier Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8664bf48-b5d4-5d30-9acf-912dfc8268a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(⟨f(x), \\cos (5x)⟩)/(⟨\\sin (5x), \\sin (5x)⟩)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164907} {:answer/id #uuid "c67cd987-6ec5-57a7-9c7c-effb0b0eca94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(⟨f(x), \\cos (5x)⟩)/(⟨\\cos (5x), \\cos (5x)⟩)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164908} {:answer/id #uuid "55adcd35-c626-5f20-b50a-cf74b96126f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(⟨f(x), \\sin (5x)⟩)/(⟨\\sin (5x), \\sin (5x)⟩)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164909} {:answer/id #uuid "6561e55b-1285-52d8-97de-2927a802d5a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(⟨\\sin (5x), \\sin (5x)⟩)/(⟨f(x), \\cos (5x)⟩)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164910} {:answer/id #uuid "674d6ba9-cecb-5505-ae5e-615d1b2906c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(⟨\\cos (5x), \\cos (5x)⟩)/(⟨f(x), \\cos (5x)⟩)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164911}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9c7846c0-b2b6-5038-9ca3-dc4dfc0646cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164906} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dc7ec21-5577-5a65-9979-79200f1a6b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164913} {:answer/id #uuid "e1bad02e-e232-53fc-8690-ea138de9d8ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}x^{2}\\sin (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164914} {:answer/id #uuid "e4298d08-8ab3-57c9-bc2a-6a66b95cd618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}x^{2}\\sin (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164915} {:answer/id #uuid "e76aa029-6c75-563f-a800-3515bb7cd888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164916} {:answer/id #uuid "ed57003e-41b1-5afc-9a4c-a046bfd233ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π}^{π}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164917} {:answer/id #uuid "f7bfe1a2-18b2-57c6-ab93-12c6304c0ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π}^{π}x^{2}\\sin (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164918}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5382fbf0-4b1a-56a6-9249-8660943fa491" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164912} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f85944a-3739-5bd3-9b9f-31e1f837c946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164920} {:answer/id #uuid "30729cbd-5e16-5d82-9c54-9c174f94c35b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164921} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1cc985-9f7d-53d0-b9b2-8e123719ef83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164922} {:answer/id #uuid "60006be5-37c7-5639-af46-811b8ab5e7b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164923} {:answer/id #uuid "6553a0b5-752e-5ac2-a4cc-fbb7b0d5719b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164924}] :answer-field/id #uuid "271d24b0-f894-5072-8a47-b0f5a9fb104a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164919}] :question/id #uuid "aa20d8e3-c4d9-5112-bd9c-ec30f6aaa6fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-340418" :question/problem "Let $F(x) = x^{2}$ for $x ∈ R$, and let $f(x)$ be the $2π$-periodic extension of $F$ from $x ∈ [- π, π)$ to $x ∈ R$. The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$f(x)∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx))$.\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient $a_{5}$ below, where $⟨, ⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[- π, π)$.\n\n$(⟨\\sin (5x), \\sin (5x)⟩)/(⟨f(x), \\cos (5x)⟩)$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ae32a2a-438f-547c-8510-7e9f760c3cb6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Periodic Extensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9028c9b4-0eb1-5e1d-8cbb-b8155bf83fdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220825} {:answer/id #uuid "42d01fd9-eb03-57e5-bc69-1b1db7f60d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220826}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d7a167be-384f-5c25-9282-cb9ba2153373" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220824} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b543458-7e54-561a-9075-86139e3b8880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220828} {:answer/id #uuid "d50e4592-528d-5165-9cb2-a253443b9bed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220829}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5cc5726b-446b-5ee4-a6c8-b77ca10a46f0" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220827} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0adb0fd-986e-5eb1-b42e-e5710d8afacf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220831} {:answer/id #uuid "3c8a4d8e-07d8-50dd-a0c9-57ba54723b07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220832}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a10dc220-6ce1-5d98-a8a8-fb09c06c9618" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220830} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f03ee50b-c228-57c5-a275-7737e1307621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220834} {:answer/id #uuid "1b1352ba-0c32-552c-a5e2-a62209416db8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220835}] :answer-field/id #uuid "04c0ac69-b2e7-5d82-8067-b78da40a0950" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220833}] :question/id #uuid "2cc2bb4c-12ce-5499-9381-7f752bdaab23" :question/math-academy-id "q-341129" :question/problem "$F(x) = {x^{2}, \\mid x ∈ [0, 3); - 3x, \\mid x ∈ [- 3, 0)$\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x ∈ [- 3, 3)$. Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x ∈ R$.\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x ∈ (-3, 3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x ∈ (-3, 3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $lim_(x → - 3^{+})F(x) = lim_(x → 3^{-})F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ba61d-2e00-539a-84d0-84b63bf92be0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Shifts From the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7688a707-770a-5d26-8326-a3a0e9adea9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fe5d17ffdb189defcd001b26eadf333df4d19157af44beeaa4c270083d1a625d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391770} {:answer/id #uuid "53a678d2-9902-5f47-87db-4b0cf14c0bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6f/6fd173c18910a9b2c428e7c4e5c52e13935a2439442610fea134ac9bb63fdf01.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391771} {:answer/id #uuid "196682d7-8bb1-57df-ac61-d81412a74a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/afbe8291979d90666756181ca898851c5f0f30755ba985c32d56bd4b65e1fa5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391772} {:answer/id #uuid "b7743ef4-153d-5bf1-9a95-2e67eec1dc0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/15/15227beae514ccbfe85f3ba5d1ac4bd47fc130c5c15ff4ecd4de1a2813f8f2db.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391773} {:answer/id #uuid "93d16724-e79f-567d-8131-03ecce9ca2c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ac/ac72ad6572e3dd3b9b75c5d7a681ab14df055031cf3b1baa3c5b0df2de7ae5a4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7688a707-770a-5d26-8326-a3a0e9adea9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fe5d17ffdb189defcd001b26eadf333df4d19157af44beeaa4c270083d1a625d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391770} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e3194f8-8dc6-5b04-9805-27bc947704b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e769abe-27f5-51cc-8d1a-a0d37ed70a2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-341512" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac72ad6572e3dd3b9b75c5d7a681ab14df055031cf3b1baa3c5b0df2de7ae5a4.png)\n\nConsider a function $f(t)$ for which the graph of the Laplace transform $L{f(t)}=F(s)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $L{{e}^{-6t}f(t)}?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{-6t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the left.\n\n![](images/fe/fe5d17ffdb189defcd001b26eadf333df4d19157af44beeaa4c270083d1a625d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab0d2c2f-e7a4-51bb-a20a-f28c2f7ded8b" :knowledge-point/title "Adding Multiples of Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96fe8ea5-6816-5080-896c-06099a363468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391777} {:answer/id #uuid "3a2e0c2f-6803-55a5-a5f2-e054623d8ce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391778} {:answer/id #uuid "29c4b51b-0698-508e-a6f7-8d02ca0c1eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391779} {:answer/id #uuid "daf1ae0f-726d-57c8-8c92-a3fce61a6349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391780} {:answer/id #uuid "0d6772bb-89f5-5ddb-bfb0-b3b493fd97bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96fe8ea5-6816-5080-896c-06099a363468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391777} :answer-field/id #uuid "48032479-0a2f-561c-a438-98d70ba6d18f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df695587-ec35-5395-87f6-af8d0149e4cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-34158" :question/problem "Let $c=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $d=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$ Find $c+d.$" :question/worked-solution "We add the vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c+d & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2-3 \\\\ 0+2 \\\\ -1+5 \\\\ 4+7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ce19559-0f08-5ef7-9ce4-58e8e14094ab" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Multiple of a Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ac916e8-03ca-50c6-a581-99efb62080da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[